wn notes: Candidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, pretentious, wrong tone. Also we have note: B has relatively weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues (but that was from Agent 3's notes). Actually our own notes include B's weak memory for numbers? In our notes: \"Candidate B has a relatively weak memory for numbers.\" Yes. Also \"Candidate B makes nasty remarks about his colleagues.\" That's in our notes? Actually that was in the candidate info list: \"Candidate B makes nasty remarks about his colleagues.\" So we have that as a concern.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education, also \"100% reliable\" and \"creates a positive atmosphere with his crew\" from Agent 1's notes (but we have those from other agents). Our own notes: \"Candidate C can make correct decisions quickly.\" \"Candidate C has difficulty communicating ideas.\" \"Candidate C is regarded as egocentric.\" \"Candidate C is not very willing to further his education.\" Also \"Candidate C keeps calm in a crisis.\" \"Candidate C understands complicated technology.\" So we have additional facts: keeps calm in crisis (stress resilience), understands complicated technology (technical competence). Good.\n\nCandidate A: we have many facts but no stress or crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate D: we have many facts but also concerns: know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nNow we need to decide vote. The panel currently has Agent 1 voting C, Agent 3 voting B, Agent 2 (us) currently voting B (from earlier). We need to respond now. We can either maintain B or shift. Let's evaluate.\n\nCriteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nCandidate B: reliability: very conscientious (good). Stress resilience: handles stress well (good). Technical competence: good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills (good). Decision quality: not explicit but likely good. Attention: weak memory for numbers (concern). Crew cooperation: pretentious, wrong tone, makes nasty remarks (serious). Professional communication: issues. Responsibility: not explicit. Adaptability: unknown.\n\nCandidate C: reliability: 100% reliable (excellent). Stress resilience: keeps calm in crisis (good). Technical competence: understands complicated technology (good). Decision quality: can make correct decisions quickly (good). Attention: excellent attention skills (from Agent 1). Crew cooperation: creates positive atmosphere with crew (good). Professional communication: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric (issues). Adaptability: not willing to further education (concern). Responsibility: not explicit but likely.\n\nThus C seems stronger overall: reliability, crew cooperation, decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence, attention. Communication issues exist but also B has serious communication issues (pretentious, nasty remarks). B also has weak memory for numbers (attention). C's egocentric and unwillingness to further education are concerns but maybe less safety-critical than B's nasty remarks.\n\nThus I think we should shift vote to Candidate C. Provide reasoning: C's reliability, crew atmosphere, decision speed, calmness, technical competence outweigh B's communication and memory issues. Also note that B's memory for numbers could affect flight planning, and nasty remarks could degrade cockpit resource management.\n\nThus we will change vote to Candidate C.\n\nWe need to reference Agent 1's argument and Agent 3's support for B. Provide explicit facts: \"100% reliable\", \"creates positive atmosphere\", \"keeps calm in crisis\", \"understands complicated technology\", \"can make correct decisions quickly\". Also note B's weak memory for numbers and nasty remarks.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate C.\n\nProceed."}
